Rem ==================================================================
Rem lineshaft.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: control flow and
Rem                    execution failure
Rem
Rem THIS MACHINE DECLARES NO WORDS. It is where the runtime's own
Rem control-flow primitives are documented: Call and Ifte, which route
Rem execution, and Error and Assert, which end it. All four are callable
Rem from any program with no Include at all — including this file gives
Rem you nothing, and is not needed to use them.
Rem
Rem THE NAME. A LINE SHAFT was the belt-and-pulley system that carried
Rem mechanical power from a mill's central steam engine out to every
Rem individual machine on the floor. That is what Call does: it routes
Rem execution from the Shoddy ENGINE — src/Shoddy.Runtime/Engine.cs is
Rem already the name of the dispatch core — out to a quotation. Naming
Rem implementation-layer machinery after period mill equipment is the
Rem established pattern here and not a novelty: the lexer's own
Rem namespace is Shoddy.Devil, after the spiked grinding machine that
Rem tore old cloth back into loose fiber (see docs/heritage.html).
Rem
Rem WHY NOT "runtime". Because "the runtime" is already this codebase's
Rem standing term for EVERY builtin collectively — math.shoddy's header
Rem says "the runtime supplies the primitives", and seedbuiltin.shoddy
Rem calls itself the bridge to "the RUNTIME's own builtins". A machine
Rem named runtime.shoddy would read as though it held the whole builtin
Rem surface, when thirteen other machines and Appendix A hold the rest
Rem of it.
Rem
Rem WHY THESE FOUR AND NOT MORE. The scope is deliberately narrow:
Rem control flow and program failure. A future Try/Catch pair, or any
Rem other exception- or control-flow-shaped builtin, would belong here
Rem rather than in a data-domain machine. What is NOT swept in is the
Rem stack shuffles, the comparisons and the logic words — Appendix A of
Rem docs/spec.html and docs/stack.html already cover the first in depth,
Rem and all three stay core. This is the execution domain specifically,
Rem not a catch-all for builtins with no other home.
Rem
Rem AND IT IS NOT A TESTING MACHINE. Assert is here because it is a way
Rem for a program to STOP, alongside Error — not because this is a
Rem quality or test-tooling domain. There is no assertion-helper library
Rem in the tree to build one around, and Error/Assert are used across
Rem two thirds of the machines as ordinary internal validation rather
Rem than as test apparatus. Words like AssertEqual or AssertClose would
Rem be a feature addition and not a home for these two.
Rem ==================================================================

Rem ---- the builtins this machine documents ---------------------------
Rem
Rem NOT Defs. The engine dispatches these, and a Def whose name is a
Rem builtin is refused. The flat reference is section 10 of
Rem docs/spec.html, and the stack effects below are the ones the
Rem compiler's own Effects.cs table declares.

Rem Call(f As [ ... -- ... ], args ...) As t          ( f -- ? )
Rem     Apply a function value to its arguments. Call(f, x) is a
Rem     one-argument quotation applied to x, Call(f, a, b) a
Rem     two-argument one, and so on — the arity is the quotation's.
Rem
Rem     THE ONE GENUINELY DYNAMIC BUILTIN. Its net stack effect is
Rem     whatever the quotation's is, which is why Effects.cs lists it
Rem     under Dynamic rather than as a fixed pair, and why the stack
Rem     checker treats any Def that reaches it as unknown rather than
Rem     guessing. Every higher-order word in the library — Map, Filter,
Rem     Fold, seq's own Any and All, eng's derivatives and integrators —
Rem     reaches execution through this word.

Rem Ifte(cond As Boolean, then As t, else As t) As t  ( c a b -- v )
Rem     Call's fixed three-argument shell, and the expression form of
Rem     If/Then/Else. Both arms are EVALUATED before the choice is made,
Rem     since it is an ordinary word and not a form the compiler treats
Rem     specially — so it is for choosing between two values already in
Rem     hand, and If/Then/Else is what guards work you do not want done.

Rem Error(why As String)                              ( why -- )
Rem     End the run, reporting why and the line it happened on. Shoddy
Rem     has no catchable errors, so this is final: nothing downstream
Rem     sees it and there is nothing to unwind to. It is the ordinary
Rem     way a library word refuses an argument it cannot honour —
Rem     str.shoddy's Split on an empty separator, matrix.shoddy on a
Rem     dimension mismatch. The guarded alternative, where a caller
Rem     should be able to carry on, is to answer the language's Result
Rem     or Option instead, which is what every Try* word in the tree
Rem     does.

Rem Assert(cond As Boolean, why As String)            ( cond why -- )
Rem     Error unless cond is True; nothing at all when it is. The same
Rem     finality, written as a precondition. The test suites under tst/
Rem     are built on it, and so is the internal checking in about two
Rem     thirds of the machines.
Rem ==================================================================
